  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/e/ed/Journey_Into_Mystery_Vol_1_95.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Odin summons Thor home to provide rain for Asgard, which has been hit by a drought.&nbsp; Thor starts the rain, which he says will last a week, after which he'll return to stop it.&nbsp; Thor then returns to Earth to help Professor Zaxton \"demonstrate the strange android which my other identity, Dr. Don Blake, invented!\"\n\nSure enough, a green android is displayed on stage to an audience of scientists, and it immediately solves math problems and shows that it can withstand a blow from Thor's hammer.&nbsp; Zaxton states that Dr. Blake gave the android unbreakable skin, some kind of plastic tissue.&nbsp; Zaxton goes on to say that an army of these ultra-intelligent androids would be invincible!\n\nWithout warning, the android's control circuit shorts out, and the android seems about to explode, but Thor wraps it with metal bands, slides his hammer into them, and hurls the android out the window, where it explodes harmlessly in Earth's upper atmosphere.\n\nThough Professor Zaxton <i>seems</i> relieved that no one was injured, Thor is clearly disgruntled, suspecting that Zaxton might have done something on purpose.&nbsp; Back in his office as Don Blake, he finds Professor Zaxton waiting for him to demonstrate a \"duplicating machine.\"&nbsp; He thinks Blake's android was a mechanical miracle, so he wants Blake's help duplicating human beings!&nbsp; Blake is horrified, but Zaxton is holding Jane Foster prisoner!&nbsp; Turns out he <i>did</i> sabotage the android because he was jealous.\n\nBlake could turn to Thor, but he decides to bide his time, and after only a \"long, long night\" in the lab, they're ready to test his machine, which he starts with an alley cat, producing multiple duplicates.&nbsp; He tells Blake where Jane is, so he turns to Thor, but Zaxton sees him (and now knows Blake is Thor) and zaps him with the duplicating ray.&nbsp; Now there are two Thors!&nbsp; The physical structure is the same, but the personalities are opposite, so the duplicate Thor protects Zaxton's machine from being smashed by Thor, and to give the duplicate an advantage, Zaxton duplicates a second hammer for Thor 2!&nbsp; Zaxton, of course, wants \"total, absolute power!\"&nbsp; He plans to use duplicate Thor to make him \"ruler of the world!\"\n\nAs Thor attempts to escape, Zaxton uses his device to create a duplicate of an entire office building for him to bump into!&nbsp; Later, he creates \"dozens of airliners\" to block Thor's path!&nbsp; Thor 2 catches up to him and hurls both hammers, which strike Thor in the chest, but he feels nothing and then notices the inscription on the hammer, which states that the bearer will only have the power of Thor if he is worthy -- the evil Thor is <i>not</i>, so he cannot harm Thor at all, and one blow from the real uru hammer is enough to send him fading away \"into the nothingness from whence he came!\"\n\nTurning back to Zaxton, the scientist desperately creates a duplicate of himself so Thor can't tell which is which, but loses his grip on the machine.&nbsp; Desperately trying to grab it, he instead falls to his death, the machine shattering as well.&nbsp; The duplicate professor has his opposite personality \"without his streak of villainy.\"&nbsp; Thor is happy that the duplicate will live to \"do good where the original did bad!\"\n\nHe rescues Jane and then, days later, returns to Asgard to stop the rain as promised.&nbsp; Odin is happy he remembered to come back, but Thor assures him that \"no matter what adventures may summon me to the world below, <i>this</i> is the land my heart calls home!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: right | DonBlakesAndroid | width=12%] Blake's invention of the android comes out of <i>nowhere</i> and seems to serve no purpose at all except to introduce Professor Zaxton, described as \"one of the world's foremost theoretical physicists.\"&nbsp; But why would Blake invent such a thing?&nbsp; And furthermore, what qualifications must a man have to be able to even do this?&nbsp; How did Blake get these qualifications?&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(The story not only doesn't bother to explain, but probably hopes that readers won't ask these questions!)]\n[BR]\n[BR] (And on another note, will we ever see this android invention <i>again</i> after this issue?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=I seriously doubt it!])\n\n[ASSET: left | CrazedZaxton | width=12%] Thor's suspicion of Zaxton as deliberately sabotaging the demonstration of his android turns out to be justified, and with the story almost half over, we learn about the \"duplicating machine,\" which is a completely silly device.&nbsp; Zaxton simply appears in Blake's office after kidnapping Jane Foster to ensure his cooperation.\n\nEven dumber is the fact that, one long night of the two of them conferring together in Dr. Blake's lab is sufficient enough time in which to perfect the duplicating device such that it can now work on living beings.&nbsp; We can only read in amazement at the brilliance of Blake and Zaxton.&nbsp; On the one hand, we have Blake inventing artificial intelligence as early as 1963 (beings powerful enough to become invincible soldiers -- I ask you -- who needs Tony Stark and his power skates when you have Dr. Donald Blake on the job?).&nbsp; On the other hand, we have the seemingly unhinged Professor Zaxton who (with only one night of Blake's time) can duplicate <i>anything</i>, be it alley cat, office building, plane... <i>whatever</i>!\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=But wait!&nbsp; There's <i>more</i>!]\n\nNot only can Zaxton duplicate living creatures, he's explicitly programmed it to give the duplicates \"the <i>opposite</i> of the original's <i>personality</i> and <i>brains</i> under my telepathic control!\"&nbsp; Why on Earth would Zaxton have done this?&nbsp; And wouldn't the brilliant Dr. Blake have seen this obvious problem while they were working together on the duplicating device?\n\nDid Zaxton really suddenly go from jealous scientist to kidnapper, then to a madman who was bent on being the ruler of the world?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=(What's <i>with</i> these people?)]&nbsp; Who even needs Dr. Doom when you have this level of diabolical ability to terrorize the world?\n\nI'm sorry, but if pages 6-9 prove anything, it's only to show how quickly this story spirals out of control!\n\n[ASSET: right | DuplicatePlanes | width=12%] And what about the duplicate office building and \"dozens\" of airplanes, complete with duplicate copies of hundreds, if not <i>thousands</i> of people?&nbsp; What chaos this must have caused!\n[BR]\n[BR] Since we know the duplicate professor will apparently be able to live on, as Thor indicates on the last page, won't these other duplicates <i>also</i> live on, sharing the same past, memories, and social security numbers as their originals, but with opposite personalities?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=Talk about <i>identity theft</i>!]\n\nWith the page count dwindling away, it was time to resolve things in a hurry, and after a self-duplicating maneuver, the evil Professor Zaxton falls to his death, and his machine is smashed.\n[ASSET: center | GoodZaxton | width=67%]\n\nHis duplicate is \"good\" (and thus can be allowed to live), Jane is rescued, and the [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved].\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Thor doesn't seem at all concerned now that the duplicate Zaxton has the knowledge that Don Blake is Thor, presumably because he is \"good\" I guess.)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | MyFather | width=12%] The book is not <i>completely</i> without any positive aspects however.&nbsp; I <i>do</i> like the fact that over the last handful of issues, we're slowly seeing Thor accepting that his true heritage is on Asgard.&nbsp; At the beginning, it seemed like Thor viewed Odin as a curiosity, and now we see him happy to be in Asgard, call Odin \"father,\" and even refer to Asgard \"the land my heart calls home\" without any reservations.&nbsp; On page 2, Thor even suggests that, were it not for his role on Earth, he would \"gladly dwell in <i>this</i> dimension <i>forever</i>!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=Very nice!]\n\nWe see more and more signs that the two identities of Thor and Blake and beginning to blend together, hopefully never again to read comments such as [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Oh, it's Odin, the legendary father of Thor!\"]&nbsp; Instead, it's transforming into phrases such as [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"my father\"] -- to the betterment of the magazine.&nbsp; This character has a unique aspect to him that no other Marvel character will ever be able to...<i>uh</i>...duplicate (<i>argh</i>, sorry for the pun).\n\nWe may rightly wonder how it is that Odin could not summon rain in Asgard without Thor's help, but <i>hey</i>, even this small bit of world-building in the opening and closing moments saves the issue from an truly low rating in the end...\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nAnother mostly sour taste in my mouth after reading this.\n\n<i>Ugh</i>, what an absolute mess of a story, and quite frankly, one with an inane and silly plot, filled with gaping holes -- totally deserving of a poor rating!&nbsp; There is now an entire office building and a dozen planes worth of duplicated humans out there that the plot never bothers to explain away.\n\nThere is no escaping the fact that this was a plot worthy of Mister Mxyzptlk!\n[BR] In other words, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=it had <b>no business</b> being in a Marvel comic!]\n\n<i>True,</i> it was partially redeemed by the brief opening and closing scenes in Asgard, which I fully acknowledge, but even these seemed stapled onto the comic and had nothing whatsoever to do with the rest of a story that had nothing else worthwhile to put forth.&nbsp; The scenes in Asgard seemed quite literally \"trapped\" inside an otherwise terrible concept.\n\nThus, the final grade would seem to be a 2.5, given that there was a <i>hint</i> of an idea present, but in this case, the rest of the story was <i>so poor</i>, that I choose instead to penalize it a half a point, which holds that the book was largely a waste of time (and I still feel this is <i>generous</i>)!\n\n[ASSET: right | LongHandles | width=20%] Finally, Joe Sinnott once again provides the art, and all I have to say about the handle on that hammer is that Thor needn't worry about getting a cane when he finally gets old, because he can just use his hammer -- that thing has to be <b>at least 3 feet long</b>!\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Good grief!]",
  "decoder_ring": "The notion that an evil Thor is not worthy to hold the hammer is interesting -- one that is clearly demonstrated here, but I'm reminded of the dramatic conclusion of the 1978 Avengers Annual 8 as well, and its final showdown between Iron Man and a \"Thunder God gone <b>berserk</b>!\"",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}